<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Kara-Khanid Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1042 CE – 1212 CE · capital Samarkand</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The western branch of the Kara-Khanid dynasty, ruling Samarkand and Bukhara under Seljuk and then Qara Khitai overlordship.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate</a> divided around 1042, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-tamghach-khan">Ibrahim Tamghach Khan</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasr-ibn-ali">Nasr ibn Ali</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> his capital, building a strong, centralised state that his descendants ruled.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-tamghach-khan">Ibrahim Tamghach Khan</a> was remembered for justice, founding hospitals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. The khans ruled a settled Persian-speaking population with Turkic troops, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> was governed in time by the hereditary Burhan family of religious leaders. In 1089 the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah</a> took Samarkand, making the khans <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> crushed the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Ahmad Sanjar</a> at the Qatwan steppe in 1141, the khans became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. The last khan, Uthman, was executed in 1212 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-ad-din-muhammad-ii">Muhammad II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> after a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side">